0083 | \begin{abstract}
0084 | We prove that a putative $[72,36,16]$ code is not the image of linear code over $\ZZ_4$, $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$ or $\FF_2+v\FF_2$, thus proving that the extremal doubly-even $[72,36,16]$ binary code cannot have an automorphism group containing a fixed point free involution. Combining this with the previously proved result by Bouyuklieva that such a code cannot have an automorphism group containing an involution with fixed points, we conclude that the automorphism group of the $[72,36,16]$ code cannot be of even order, leaving $1$, $3$ and $5$ as the only possibilities.
0085 | \end{abstract}
0086 | 
0087 | 
0088 | \section{Introduction}
0089 | A long standing open problem asks whether a binary $[72,36,16]$ Type~II code exists, where Type~II indicates that the code is self-dual and the Hamming weight of all vectors in the code are congruent to $0 \pmod{4}.$  The question has remained open over 40 years and is the first in  a series of open questions about the existence of $[24k,12k,4k+4]$ Type~II codes. A number of attempts have been made to solve this problem, many of which have led to restrictions about the automorphism group of such a code if it exists. We can refer the reader to such works as \cite{Bouyuklieva}, \cite{Boyuklieva-2}, \cite{Nebe}, \cite{OBrien}, \cite{Yankov} and the references therein. The latest works indicate that the automorphism group of a doubly-even $[72,36,16]$ code has order $5$ or divides $24$.
0090 | 
0091 | Recently in \cite{Kier}, Kiermaier showed that the Gray image of a self-dual $\ZZ_4$ code cannot have parameters $(72, 2^{36},16)$.
0092 | 
0093 | In this paper we shall build on this and show that the extremal doubly-even $[72,36,16]$ code is not the Gray image of any linear code over $\ZZ_4$, $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$ or $\FF_2+v\FF_2$. In particular, this will show that the automorphism group of this code cannot have a fixed point  free involution. Bouyuklieva, in \cite{Bouyuklieva} showed that the automorphism group of the putative $[72,36,16]$ code cannot contain an automorphism of order $2$ with a positive number of fixed points. Combining the two results, we conclude that the automorphism group of the $[72,36,16]$ code cannot contain an automorphism of order 2, hence eliminating all even orders, leaving only $1$, $3$ and $5$ as the possibilities for the order of the automorphism group of such a code.
0094 | 
0095 | There are four rings of order 4, namely $\ZZ_4$, $\FF_4$, $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$, $u^2=0$ and $\FF_2 +  v \FF_2,$ $v^2=v.$
0096 | We will focus on the three rings that are not a field.
0097 | 
0098 | Each of these rings has a Gray map as given in the following:
0099 | $$\begin{array}{cccc}
0100 | \ZZ_4 & \FF_2 + u \FF_2 & \FF_2 + v \FF_2 & \FF_2^2 \\
0101 | \hline
0102 | 0  & 0 & 0 & 00  \\
0103 | 1& 1&  v & 01 \\
0104 | 2&u&  1 & 11 \\
0105 | 3&1+u&   1+v& 10 \\
0106 | \end{array}
0107 | $$
0108 | Denote the Gray map for $\ZZ_4$ by $\phi_4$.
0109 | For $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$, denote the map by $\phi_u$ and define it  as  $\phi_u(a+bu) = (b,a+b).  $
0110 | For $\FF_2+ v \FF_2$ denote the map  by $\phi_v$ and define it  as $\phi_v(a+bv) = (a,a+b).$
0111 | The image under any of these maps is known as the Gray image of the code.  The map $\phi_4$ is not linear but $\phi_u$ and $\phi_v$ are linear.
0112 | 
0113 | The Lee weight of an element is the Hamming weight of its image under the Gray map.
0114 | Then for a vector the Lee weight is the sum of the Lee weights of its components.  The Lee weight enumerator for a code of length $n$  is defined as $L_C(x,y) = \sum_{\vc \in C} x^{N - wt(\vc)} y^{wt(\vc)}$ where $N=2n$ and $wt(\vc)$ is the Lee weight of the vector $\vc.$ Notice that the Hamming weight enumerator of the Gray image is simply the Lee weight enumerator for the code.
0115 | 
0116 | 
0117 | The inner-product is defined as: $[\vv,\vw] = \sum v_i w_i.$
0118 | Define $C^\perp = \{ \vw \ | \ [\vw,\vv]=0, \forall \vv \in C \}.$
0119 | When we need to differentiate in which ring the inner-product is being calculated we shall use
0120 | $[\vv,\vw]_2$,
0121 | $[\vv,\vw]_4$,
0122 | $[\vv,\vw]_u$ and
0123 | $[\vv,\vw]_v$.
0124 | A code $C$ is self-dual if $C=C^\perp$ and is self-orthogonal if $C \subseteq C^\perp.$
0125 | 
0126 | We define a formally self-dual code as a code $C$ such that $L_C(x,y) = L_{C^\perp}(x,y).$
0127 | It is said to be Type~I if it has only even weights with at least one vector of weight $2 \pmod{4}.$  The code is said to be Type~II if all weights in the code are congruent to $0 \pmod{4}.$
0128 | 
0129 | \begin{thm}\label{images}
0130 | The Gray image of a formally self-dual code  over $\ZZ_4$, $\FF_2+ u \FF_2$ or $\FF_2 +v \FF_2$ is a binary formally self-dual code.
0131 | The Gray image of a Type~II formally self-dual code over $\FF_2 +u \FF_2 $ or $\FF_2+v\FF_2$ is a binary self-dual doubly-even code.
0132 | \end{thm}
0133 | \begin{proof}
0134 | The result follows from the definition of Type~II and the fact that Lee weight enumerator is equal to the Hamming weight enumerator of its Gray image.
0135 | \end{proof}
0136 | 
0137 | The image of a self-dual code over $\ZZ_4$ can be non-linear or linear, and if it is linear it can be either self-dual or not self-dual.  The image of a self-dual code over $\FF_2 +u \FF_2$ or $\FF_2 + v \FF_2$ is a linear self-dual code.     See \cite{f2uf2} and \cite{AK} for details.  For a description of self-dual codes over $\ZZ_4$ see \cite{CS} and \cite{Pless}.  The images of self-dual codes over $\ZZ_4$ are described in \cite{cristina}.
0138 | 
0139 | The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section 2, we will talk about the torsion codes over $\ZZ_4$ and $\FF_2+u\FF_2$ and study some of the properties. In Section 3, we will show that the $[72,36,16]$-extremal code cannot be the Gray image of a linear code over $\ZZ_4$ or $\FF_2+u\FF_2$. In Section 4, we will prove that it cannot be the image of a linear code over $\FF_2+v\FF_2$.
