0142 | \section{Torsion Codes}
0143 | 
0144 | In this section we shall describe Torsion codes over $\ZZ_4$ and $\FF_2+u\FF_2$.
0145 | 
0146 | A code over $\ZZ_4$ has a generator matrix of the following form:
0147 | \begin{equation}
0148 | \left( \begin{array}{ccc}
0149 | I_{d_0} & A & B_1 + 2B_2 \\
0150 | 0 & 2 I_{d_1} & 2D \\
0151 | \end{array} \right),
0152 | \end{equation}
0153 | where $A,B_1,B_2, D$ are binary matrices.
0154 | The code then has $4^{d_0} 2^{d_1}$ elements.
0155 | 
0156 | Likewise, a code over $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$ has a generator matrix of the following form:
0157 | \begin{equation}
0158 | \left( \begin{array}{ccc}
0159 | I_{d_0} & A & B_1 + uB_2 \\
0160 | 0 & u I_{d_1} & uD \\
0161 | \end{array} \right),
0162 | \end{equation}
0163 | where $A,B_1,B_2, D$ are binary matrices.
0164 | The code then has $4^{d_0} 2^{d_1}$ elements.
0165 | 
0166 | 
0167 | 
0168 | For $\ZZ_4$ we define $Tor_0(C) = \{ \vv \in \FF_2^n \ | \ \exists\  \vw \in \FF_2^n, \vv+2\vw \in C \}$ and
0169 | $Tor_1(C) = \{ \vv \in \FF_2^n \ | \ 2\vv \in C \}.$
0170 | For $\FF_2+u\FF_2$ we define $Tor_0(C) = \{ \vv \in \FF_2^n \ | \ \exists\  \vw \in \FF_2^n, \vv+u\vw \in C \}$ and
0171 | $Tor_1(C) = \{ \vv \in \FF_2^n \ | \ u\vv \in C \}.$
0172 | For both the binary code $Tor_0(C) $ is generated by
0173 | \begin{equation}
0174 | \left( \begin{array}{ccc}
0175 | I_{d_0} & A & B_1  \\
0176 | \end{array} \right)
0177 | \end{equation}
0178 | and the binary code $Tor_1(C)$ is generated by
0179 | \begin{equation}
0180 | \left( \begin{array}{ccc}
0181 | I_{d_0} & A & B_1  \\
0182 | 0 &  I_{d_1} & D \\
0183 | \end{array} \right).
0184 | \end{equation}
0185 | 
0186 | It follows immediately that $Tor_0(C) \subseteq Tor_1(C)$  and that $|C| = |Tor_0(C)| |Tor_1(C)|.$
0187 | 
0188 | \begin{thm}
0189 | For codes over $\ZZ_4$ and $\FF_2+u\FF_2$, $Tor_0(C) = Tor_1(C)^\perp$ if and only if $C$ is a self-dual code of length $n$.
0190 | \end{thm}
0191 | \begin{proof}
0192 | The result for self-dual codes is well known for both rings, see \cite{Pless} and \cite{f2uf2}.
0193 | 
0194 | We shall show the other direction for $\ZZ_4$, the proof for $\FF_2+u\FF_2$ is the same replacing $u$ for $2$.
0195 | Now assume $Tor_0(C) = Tor_1(C)^\perp.$    This gives that $|Tor_0(C)| |Tor_1(C)| = 2^n.$ Hence $|C| = 2^n$ and so has the size of a self-dual code.
0196 | Let $\vv \in Tor_0(C)$ and $\vw \in Tor_1(C)$.  We have that $[\vv,\vw]_2=0$ since the codes are orthogonal.
0197 | Then
0198 | $[\vv + 2 \vv',2 \vw ]_4 = 2[\vv,\vw]_4 + [2\vv',2\vw]_4 =0$.  Hence the code is self-dual.
0199 | \end{proof}
0200 | 
0201 | 
0202 | If we have a formally self-dual code over $\FF_2+u\FF_2$ or $\ZZ_4$ it is necessary to have    $$|Tor_0(C)| |Tor_1(C)| = 2^n$$ but we need a vector $\vv \in Tor_0(C)$ and $\vw \in Tor_1(C)$ such that $[\vv,\vw]_2 \neq 0.$
0203 | 
0204 | 
0205 | We can now give a generalization of Lemma~{3.2} in \cite{Kier}.
0206 | 
0207 | \begin{lem} \label{like32}
0208 | Let $C$ be a self-dual code over $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$  of even length   $n$ and minimum Lee distance $d$.
0209 | Then there exists a binary self-dual $[n,\frac{n}{2}]$ code $D$ such that the minimum distance of $D_0$ is at least $\frac{d}{2}$, where $D_0$ is the dual of the subcode of doubly-even vectors of $D$.
0210 | \end{lem}
0211 | \begin{proof}
0212 | The proof follows as in \cite{Kier}.  Specifically, we have $Tor_0(C)$ is a self-orthogonal binary code. It is well known that there is a binary self-dual code $D$ containing $Tor_0(C)$.  We know $Tor_0(C)$ is doubly-even, and it is contained in $D_0$, so $(D_0^\perp) \subseteq (Tor_0(C))^\perp.$
0213 | But $(Tor_0(C))^\perp = Tor_1(C)$, and the minimum Hamming weight of $Tor_0(C) $ is less than or equal to the minimum Lee weight of $C$, which is less than or equal to $2 d_H(Tor_1(C)).$ This gives the result.
0214 | \end{proof}
