0216 |  \section{Images of Codes}
0217 | 
0218 |  It is well known that in a binary linear code where all weights are doubly-even, the code must be self-orthogonal.
0219 |  This follows from the fact that if the sum of two doubly-even vectors is doubly-even they must be orthogonal.
0220 |  Over $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$  and $\ZZ_4$ this is not true.  For example, consider the vectors $(1+u,1+u,1+u,1+u,0,0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1,0, 1,1,1,u)$. The first vector has weight 4 and the second has weight 8, their sum is
0221 |  $(u,u,u,1+u,1,1,1,u)$ which has weight 12.  However, the inner-product of the two vectors is $1+u \neq 0.$
0222 |  For $\ZZ_4$ do the same to the vectors $(1,1,1,1,0,0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1,0,2,1,1,1).$
0223 | 
0224 |  In \cite{f2uf2}, it is stated that $C$ is self-dual if and only if $\phi_u(C)$ is self-dual.  The proof, however, needs to be corrected, since it states that if the image of two vectors are orthogonal their their preimages are as well.  However, consider $(1)$ and $(1+u)$, their images are orthogonal but the vectors are not.  We now state a correct proof of this result.
0225 | 
0226 |  \begin{thm}\label{first}
0227 |  Let $C$ be a code over $\FF_2+ u\FF_2$.  Then $C$ is self-dual if and only if $\phi_u(C)$ is a binary self-dual code.
0228 |  \end{thm}
0229 |  \begin{proof}
0230 |  It follows easily from the linearity of $\phi_u$ that if $C$ is self-dual then $\phi_u(C)$ is self-dual, see \cite{f2uf2}.
0231 | 
0232 |  Next we handle the other direction.  Assume that $\phi_u(C)$ is a binary self-dual code.
0233 |  Let $\vc_1,\vc_2 \in C$, with $\vc_1 = \vx_1 + u\vy_1$, $\vc_2 = \vx_2 + u\vy_2$, where $\vx_i,\vy_i$ are binary vectors.
0234 |  Then, we have that the image is permutation equivalent to the vectors $(\vy_1,\vx_1+\vy_1)$  and
0235 |  $(\vy_2,\vx_2+\vy_2)$.  So we will simply say that  $\phi_u(\vc_1) = (\vy_1,\vx_1+\vy_1)$  and
0236 |  $\phi_u(\vc_2) = (\vy_2,\vx_2+\vy_2)$.
0237 | 
0238 |  Then we have that $[\phi_u(\vc_1),\phi_u(\vc_2)]_2 = 0$ which implies that
0239 |  \begin{equation}\label{eq1}[\vy_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 + [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vy_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vx_2,\vy_1]_2 =0.
0240 |  \end{equation}
0241 |  Simplifying gives that
0242 |   \begin{equation}\label{eq2} [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 + [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2  + [\vx_2,\vy_1]_2 =0.
0243 |  \end{equation}
0244 | 
0245 |  Now consider the inner-product in $\FF_2+u\FF_2$:
0246 |  \begin{equation}\label{eq3}
0247 |  [\vc_1,\vc_2]_u = [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 + u ([\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vx_2,\vy_1]_2).
0248 | \end{equation}
0249 |  If we show that $ [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 =0$ this implies that $ [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2  + [\vx_2,\vy_1]_2 =0$ and we have that
0250 |  $ [\vc_1,\vc_2]_u =0.$
0251 | 
0252 |  The vectors $\vc_1 + \vc_2 = (\vx_1 + \vx_2)  + u (\vy_1 + \vy_2)$ and $u \vc_1 = \vx_1 u$ are both elements of $C$ since it is linear.
0253 | 
0254 |  Then we have that
0255 |  \begin{equation}
0256 |  [ \phi_u(\vc_1),\phi_u(u\vc_1)]_2 = [ (\vy_1,\vx_1+\vy_1), (\vx_1,\vx_1)]_2 =
0257 |  [\vx_1,\vy_1]_2 +   [\vx_1,\vx_1]_2 +  [\vx_1,\vy_1]_2 =  [\vx_1,\vx_1]_2 =0.
0258 |  \end{equation}
0259 |  Then
0260 |  \begin{eqnarray*}
0261 |  0= [\phi_u(\vc_1 + \vc_2), \phi_u(u \vc_1) ]_2 &=&
0262 |  [(\vy_1+\vy_2, \vx_1+\vx_2 + \vy_1+\vy_2),(\vx_1,\vx_1)]_2 \\
0263 |  &=&
0264 | [\vx_1,\vy_1]_2+
0265 | [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 +
0266 | [\vx_1,\vx_1]_2 +
0267 | [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 +
0268 | [\vx_1,\vy_1]_2 +
0269 | [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2  \\
0270 |  &=& [\vx_1,\vx_1]_2  + [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2.
0271 |  \end{eqnarray*}
0272 |  Then since $[\vx_1,\vx_1]_2 =0$ we have that $[\vx_1,\vx_2]_2=0$ and we have the result.
0273 |  \end{proof}
0274 | 
0275 | 
0276 |  \begin{cor}
0277 |  If $C$ is a Type~II formally self-dual code over $\FF_2+\FF_2$ then $C$ is self-dual.
0278 |  \end{cor}
0279 |  \begin{proof}
0280 | If $C$ is a Type~II formally self-dual code over $\FF_2+\FF_2$ then by Theorem~\ref{images} we have that $\phi_u(C)$ is a Type~II binary formally self-dual code.  However, any doubly-even linear binary code must be self-orthogonal, hence it is self-dual since it has the proper cardinality.  Therefore, by Theorem~\ref{first}, the code $C$ must be self-dual.
0281 |  \end{proof}
0282 | 
0283 |  The full result of Theorem~\ref{first} is not true for codes over $\ZZ_4$.  That is if $C$ is a self-dual code over $\ZZ_4$ its image may not be self-dual, in fact, it may not even be linear.  However, we can say the following.
0284 | 
0285 |  \begin{thm}\label{second}
0286 |   Let $C$ be a code over $\ZZ_4$. If $\phi_4(C)$ is a binary self-dual code then $C$ is a quaternary self-dual code.
0287 |   \end{thm}
0288 |  \begin{proof}
0289 |  The proof follows similarly to the proof of Theorem~\ref{first}.  We begin by noticing that the every vector $\vc$ over $\ZZ_4$ can be viewed as $\vx + 2\vy$, with the Gray map defined as $\phi_4(\vc) = (\vy, \vx+\vy).$
0290 |  Then replace $u$ with $2$ and the proof follows exactly.
0291 |   \end{proof}
0292 | 
0293 | 
0294 |  In \cite{Kier}, Kiermaier proved that the image of a self-dual code over $\ZZ_4$ cannot have parameters $(72,2^{36},16)$.
0295 |  This proof can be modified to prove the following.
0296 | 
0297 |  \begin{thm}\label{fourth}
0298 | If $C$ is a self-dual code over $\FF_2+u\FF_2$  then $\phi_u(C)$ is not a $[72,36,16]$ self-dual code.
0299 | \end{thm}
0300 | \begin{proof}
0301 | The proof follows exactly as the proof in the $\ZZ_4$ case since we have the same results as given in the Torsion section.  Specifically, let $\phi_u(C)$ be a putative $[72,36,16]$ Type~II code. Then $C$ has length 36 and by Lemma~\ref{like32}, there exists a self-dual binary code $D$, with $D_0$ the subcode of doubly-even vectors, such that $D_0^\perp$ has minimum weight at least 8. But $D \subseteq D_0^\perp$ and $D$ can have minimum weight at most 8 by the standard bound from invariant theory, i.e.
0302 | $d \leq \left\{ \begin{array}{cc}   4 \lfloor \frac{n}{24} \rfloor + 6 & n \equiv 22 \pmod{24} \\
0303 | 4 \lfloor \frac{n}{24}\rfloor + 4 & {\rm \ otherwise.} \end{array} \right.$
0304 | This gives that the minimum weight of $D$ is 8.
0305 | 
0306 | The $[36,18,8]$ codes have been classified in \cite{Melchor}. Kiermaier checks in \cite{Kier}, that none of these has the correct weight for $D_0^\perp.$
0307 | \end{proof}
