0310 | \begin{thm} \label{big} A putative binary $[72,36,16]$ code is not the image of a linear code over $\FF_2+u \FF_2$ or $\ZZ_4$.
0311 | \end{thm}
0312 | \begin{proof}
0313 | Let $C$ be a code over $\ZZ_4$ or $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$ whose image under the respective Gray map is the putative $[72,36,16]$ binary self-dual code.  Then the code $C$ must be a formally self-dual code since the Lee weight enumerator is the Hamming weight enumerator of a self-dual code and hence invariant under the MacWilliams relations.
0314 | Then by Theorem~\ref{first} and Theorem~\ref{second} the code $C$ must be a self-dual code.  Then Theorem~{1.1} in \cite{Kier} and Theorem~\ref{fourth} gives a contradiction since no self-dual code has as its image the specified binary code.
0315 | \end{proof}
0316 | 
0317 | 
0318 | \begin{cor}
0319 | A putative self-dual $[72,36,16]$ Type~II code cannot have a fixed point free involution.
0320 | \end{cor}
0321 | \begin{proof}
0322 | If the code had a fixed point free involution it would be the image of a linear $\FF_2 + u \FF_2$ code, which contradicts Theorem~\ref{big}.
0323 | \end{proof}
0324 | 
0325 | Combining this with the result in \cite{Bouyuklieva} and the latest update in \cite{Nebe} we obtain the following important corollary:
0326 | \begin{cor}
0327 | The automorphism group of the doubly-even binary $[72,36,16]$ code cannot be of even order, hence it can only have order $1$, $3$ or $5$. In other words the automorphism group of the putative $[72,36,16]$ code is trivial,  $C_3$ or $C_5$.
0328 | \end{cor}
