0340 | \section{Codes over $\dbl{F}_2+v \dbl{F}_2$  }
0341 | 
0342 | In \cite{AK}, it is shown that $C$ is a self-dual code over $\FF_2+v\FF_2$ if and only if $C = CRT(C_1,C_2)$ where
0343 | $C_1$ and $C_2$ are binary self-dual codes, where $CRT$ is the map induced by the Chinese Remainder Theorem.
0344 | 
0345 | 
0346 | \begin{thm}
0347 | Let $C$ be a code over $\FF_2 + v \FF_2$.  The code $C$ is self-dual if and only if $\phi(C)$ is a binary self-dual code.
0348 | \end{thm}
0349 | \begin{proof}
0350 | The fact that if $C$ is self-dual then $\phi(C)$ is self-dual follows easily from the fact that $\phi_v$ is linear, see \cite{AK} for details.
0351 | 
0352 | Assume $\phi_v(C)$ is self-dual. Then $C$ must at least be formally self-dual since its image has the weight enumerator of a self-dual code.  So we need to show that the code is self-orthogonal and it implies that it is a self-dual code.  Let $\vc_1, \vc_2 \in C.$  We need to show that $[\vc_1,\vc_2]_v=0.  $
0353 | We can write  $\vc_1 = \vx_1 + v\vy_1$, $\vc_2 = \vx_2 + v\vy_2$, where $\vx_i,\vy_i$ are binary vectors.
0354 |  Then, we have that the image is permutation equivalent to the vectors $(\vx_1,\vx_1+\vy_1)$  and
0355 |  $(\vx_2,\vx_2+\vy_2)$.  So we will simply say that  $\phi_v(\vc_1) = (\vx_1,\vx_1+\vy_1)$  and
0356 |  $\phi_v(\vc_2) = (\vx_2,\vx_2+\vy_2)$.
0357 | 
0358 |   Then we have that $[\phi_v(\vc_1),\phi_v(\vc_2)]_2 = 0$ which implies that
0359 |  \begin{equation}\label{eq1v}[\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 + [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 + [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vy_1,\vx_2]_2 + [\vy_1,\vy_2]_2 =0.
0360 |  \end{equation}
0361 |  Simplifying gives that
0362 |   \begin{equation}\label{eq2v} [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vy_1,\vx_2]_2  + [\vy_1,\vy_2]_2 =0.
0363 |  \end{equation}
0364 |   Now consider the inner-product in $\FF_2+v\FF_2$.
0365 |  \begin{equation}\label{eq3}
0366 |  [\vc_1,\vc_2]_v = [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 + v ([\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 + [\vx_2,\vy_1]_2 +  [\vy_1,\vy_2]_2).
0367 | \end{equation}
0368 |  If we show that $ [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 =0$ this implies that $ [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2  + [\vx_2,\vy_1]_2 +  [\vy_1,\vy_2]_2=0$ and we have that
0369 |  $ [\vc_1,\vc_2]_v =0.$
0370 | 
0371 |  We know that $v \vc_1$ and $v \vc_2$ are both in $C$, since $C$ is linear.  Then
0372 |  $v \vc_1 = (\vx_1+\vy_1)v$ and  $v \vc_2 = (\vx_2+\vy_2)v.$
0373 |  Then
0374 |  \begin{eqnarray*}
0375 |  0 &=& [\phi_v(v\vc_1),\phi_v(v\vc_2) ]_2 = [(0,\vx_1+\vy_1),(0,\vx_2+\vy_2)]_2 \\
0376 |  &=&
0377 |  [\vx_1,\vx_2]_2 +
0378 |   [\vx_1,\vy_2]_2 +
0379 |    [\vy_1,\vx_2]_2 +
0380 |     [\vy_1,\vy_2]_2.
0381 |  \end{eqnarray*}
0382 | 
0383 |  Then by using Equation~\ref{eq2v} we have that $[\vx_1,\vx_2]_2=0$ and we have the result.
0384 |  \end{proof}
